# ant capture v1: <seconds> <device_type> <device_number> <8 payload bytes>
# handmade-multichannel.antcap - two power meters, one capture.
# 
# Built by tools/ant_sim.py's dry run (no radio, no room) and then cut:
# every packet here is one the simulator produced, and the only edit is
# that some page 0x10 packets were deleted. That is the whole point - the
# exact-loss figure counts what is missing from the transmitter's OWN event
# counter, so a deleted packet leaves a hole the counter still numbers, and
# the two streams must be counted separately or the holes pool.
# 
#   #4660  40 s at 8182 ticks, 160 packets, 1 page 0x10 deleted
#   #4661  40 s at 8182 ticks, 160 packets, 6 page 0x10 deleted
# 
# Only data pages were deleted, never a common page: a common page is what
# the analyser votes on to decide whether the counter steps per message or
# per page, and deleting those would make the vector test the vote instead
# of the per-channel accounting it is here for.
# 
# Replay it with the channel pairing the operator used, since a capture
# records the sensor and not the receive channel:
# 
#   python tools/ant_verify.py --replay tools/vectors/handmade-multichannel.antcap \
#       --channel 0 --device-number 4660 --channel 1 --device-number 4661
# 
# The recipe, so this can be rebuilt rather than patched: for each stream,
# ant_sim.SENSORS['power'](index, device_number, 5, Signal(100, 3, rng),
# Signal(80, 3, rng)) with rng = random.Random(11 + index), 40 s of
# dry_run(), then delete every Nth record that carries page 0x10 - N = 90
# for #4660 and N = 20 for #4661. tools/test_ant_verify.py asserts the
# resulting counts, so a rebuild that changes them is caught, not absorbed.
